You're misreading me or you don't understand your armor.  Dwarvish
boots, dwarvish helm, and dwarvish shield are all base ac:2 items,
where the more complex analysis (which still shows that on average
you're better off enchanting than rustproofing at least until they're
all +3) holds.

That's even assuming you use a scroll on those, which is wasteful.
Remember, on average enchanting gets you +2 points (on items less than
+3).

Given your items, you could:
1. Enchant a dwarvish item.  Average +2 now, average +0 after rust
attack, but you can't easily replace the 2 points lost.
2. Rustproof a dwarvish item.  Average +0 now and after rust attack.
3. Enchant the gloves/robe.  Average +2 now, average +0 after a rust
attack on one of the dwarvish items, but you can pretty easily replace
the rusted item and be back to +2.

Also, you're likely to be wearing the gloves at ascension and almost
sure to be wearing the robe.

The shield, helm, and boots are all going to get ditched soonish for
replacements (as soon as you find a small shield/shield of
reflection/elvish shield, a helm of brilliance/elvish leather or maybe
telpathy, or boots of speed/jumping).

So using the enchant armor on the gloves/robe is as good as any
alternative on the dwarvish items in the short term, better post-rust
attack (since it gives you easy flexibility to get back up to +1 or +2
quickly if you find, say, an orcish helm or another dwarvish one to
replace the rusty item), and better in the long term (when you'll be
ditching the sub-par stuff).

Most importantly though, you need to stop just letting rust monsters
rust all your stuff.  If you aren't paying attention, come around the
corner, and get hit 1-2 times before you get a chance to move that's
one thing, but standing toe-to-toe and letting it hit you in melee when
you have a trivial solution is just poor planning.



